WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.520
While many sitcom stars have gone on&nbsp;
to continue in many films and TV shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.000
others have decided to move on with their lives.&nbsp;
Though we'll always remember them for their roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
here are the real reasons you don't&nbsp;
hear from these sitcom stars anymore.

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:20.000
Ross Bagley appeared in the final two seasons of&nbsp;
"Fresh Prince of Bel-Air" as Nicky, the youngest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.480
son of the Banks family. Though he was the&nbsp;
smallest member of the cast, he left a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.760
impact, be it mocking Uncle Phil's age or just&nbsp;
goofing around with Will. He also spent time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.880
with Will Smith the actor outside of Bel-Air&nbsp;
on the set of "Independence Day" as his son.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.520
Since then, he's mostly stayed away from&nbsp;
Hollywood. Is he another child star burnout?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.680
Nope, he's living a normal life&nbsp;
in California doing normal things.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.640
After leaving acting, he grew up and went to&nbsp;
college at California State University-Northridge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.720
He's been working as a realtor in California&nbsp;
for real estate company Keller Williams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.560
selling homes in the Los Angeles area. He&nbsp;
also DJs there under the name “DJ Ro$$y B.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.560
In between all of this, he's raising a&nbsp;
son who looks like a carbon copy of him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.640
from the "Fresh Prince" days. And on top of&nbsp;
everything, his LinkedIn shows him as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.400
director of Pathways Consulting, a corporate&nbsp;
finance and IT recruiting firm. Busy guy!

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.800
Bagley wasn't asked to reprise his role in&nbsp;
"Independence Day: Resurgence." He ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.600
up getting the last laugh, though. The movie had&nbsp;
poor reviews and underwhelmed at the box office.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.440
Eric Lutes played Del Cassidy, greeting card&nbsp;
proprietor and on-again-off-again boyfriend of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.800
the titular "Caroline In The City." It remains&nbsp;
the most high profile role of his career. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.880
"Caroline" was canceled, he starred in the&nbsp;
short lived Mary-Kate and Ashley TV series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.080
"So Little Time." This marked the second time he&nbsp;
played the father of the twins and has only worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.200
sparingly since. That's in part because of a&nbsp;
health scare that saw him readjust his life.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.920
In 2013, Lutes contracted Lyme disease and malaria&nbsp;
after a tick bite. The recovery lasted two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.480
during which he lost a lot of his strength and&nbsp;
suffered immense joint pain. During this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:04.000
to aid with his recovery, he changed his exercise&nbsp;
routine, his diet, and his priorities. He credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.760
his recovery with making him a more compassionate&nbsp;
person. His inability to do much besides sit also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.120
saw him dive into one of his true passions:&nbsp;
painting. In 2020, he told Encourage TV,

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.160
"I couldn't really go to the beach,&nbsp;
because you have to stay out of the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.400
so I would paint what I wanted to be in."

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.160
He sent the paintings to a gallery where&nbsp;
they sold well, and that became his main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.200
focus. The move to painting isn't all that huge&nbsp;
a jump. His father was an accomplished artist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.760
and Eric himself has always found comfort&nbsp;
in it. He told The Chariho Times in 2020,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.600
"Deep down I've always been an artist.&nbsp;
I trusted my ability in painting more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.280
than my ability in acting. I never&nbsp;
doubted my ability to sell a painting."

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.160
Fans of Danielle Brisebois fit&nbsp;
by and large into two camps:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.320
those who know her best as a child star,&nbsp;
and those who know her best as a musician.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.920
For those in the former camp, here's&nbsp;
what happened to Stephanie Mills.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.600
Brisebois played Molly, an orphan, in the&nbsp;
original Broadway staging of "Annie." In 1978,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.760
one year after "Annie," she joined "All in&nbsp;
the Family'' as Stephanie, the niece of Archie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.880
and Edith. She warms the cold heart of Archie&nbsp;
Bunker, quickly becoming a surrogate daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.120
She stuck around through sequel and spinoff&nbsp;
series "Archie Bunker's Place," where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.760
audience watched her grow from a cute kid to a&nbsp;
teenager. She acted for a few years after "Archie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.480
Bunker's Place" went off the air, but largely&nbsp;
retired from acting to pursue her other passion.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.040
She told the Los Angeles Times in 2015,

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.960
"I found out that after doing the TV show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.560
I wanted to do music and more&nbsp;
than anything to write music."

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.600
Brisebois soon met musician Gregg Alexander,&nbsp;
and the two formed a partnership that would last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.960
decades. She performed backup vocals&nbsp;
on his 1992 album "Intoxifornication."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.240
In turn, Alexander produced and co-wrote&nbsp;
Briesbois' solo debut album "Arrive All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:51.600
Over You." The alt-rock album was a minor hit&nbsp;
in Europe and has a cult following in America.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.320
Her career as a musician reached its peak when&nbsp;
she and Alexander formed the New Radicals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.160
the short lived rock band best known&nbsp;
for hit single "You Get What You Give."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.840
Alexander ended the band not long after&nbsp;
the single charted, but their creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.400
partnership has continued. Alexander produced&nbsp;
her second album and co-wrote the music for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.000
the 2013 Keira Knightley and Mark Ruffalo film&nbsp;
"Begin Again." For this, they nabbed an Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.880
nomination for Best Achievement in Music Written&nbsp;
for Motion Pictures for the song "Lost Stars."

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.600
On her own, Brisebois has been a successful&nbsp;
songwriter. The most notable examples are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.840
hit singles "Unwritten" and "Pocketful&nbsp;
of Sunshine" by Natasha Bedingfield.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.520
Rebeca Arthur played Mary Anne on "Perfect&nbsp;
Strangers” from the second season to the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.480
finale. Mary Anne — with her recognizable curly&nbsp;
hair and high pitched voice — fell in love with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.280
Balki, married him, and had his child. Rebeca,&nbsp;
however, didn't do much in the way of acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.280
after the series concluded — entirely by choice,&nbsp;
after marrying and becoming Rebeca Arthur Golden.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.840
She told the Perfect Strangers Reviewed blog&nbsp;
in 2020 that after “Perfect Strangers” ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.360
she was still working but had lost her&nbsp;
love for it. She had gotten married and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.600
had her first daughter, which needed to&nbsp;
become a higher priority. She explained,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.160
“With acting, it was all&nbsp;
about me. But with motherhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.880
it was all about them. So, I chose them."

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.080
Arthur also shared that she has&nbsp;
kept busy with volunteer work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.600
and her clothing business, which she&nbsp;
is very vocal about on Instagram.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.320
She added that she enjoys what she’s doing and has&nbsp;
no regrets about moving on from her acting roles.

00:05:19.578 --> 00:05:24.880
Lauren Lane played C.C. Babcock, Maxwell's&nbsp;
work associate and the frequent antagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.000
of Nanny Fine on "The Nanny." Lane told Vice&nbsp;
earlier this year that her specialty was, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.600
"evil vixen" roles, an apt&nbsp;
summary of the C.C. character.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.240
After "The Nanny" got canceled, Lane found&nbsp;
her career stifled by two factors. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.400
casting directors only seeing her as&nbsp;
C.C., and second, the fact that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.680
was pushing 40 in an industry where that's not&nbsp;
a friendly milestone for women. She told Vice,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.800
"If you're Jessica Lange, you can work through&nbsp;
your 40s and 50s. But the only way you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.520
is if you have an Academy Award. That's reality."

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.560
“Sticks and stones can break my&nbsp;
bones, but bad reviews can kill you.”

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.200
In the early 2000s, she auditioned&nbsp;
for a trendy teen soap as a mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.840
She recalls nailing the audition but realizing the&nbsp;
young white male writers didn't connect with her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.160
This was a turning point. Realizing that&nbsp;
it wasn't worth struggling for roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.520
she moved to Austin, Texas, and&nbsp;
focused on raising her daughter.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.080
While she's barely been on screen since "The&nbsp;
Nanny," she hasn't exactly left acting behind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.240
She's a tenured theater professor at Texas&nbsp;
State University, and often performs in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.000
plays across the country. Lane is also&nbsp;
considering a return to screen acting:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.560
she'll be qualified to retire from academia&nbsp;
around 2023, and the time feels right to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.280
her. When asked if she'd be interested in a&nbsp;
"Nanny" reboot, her answer is a resounding

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.560
"Hell yes, I would do it."

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:45.520
In 1994, Holly Fulger had a supporting role in the&nbsp;
notable sitcom “Ellen.” She played Holly Jamison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.840
in the first season, a quirky best friend, before&nbsp;
getting written out of the show the next year.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.480
Fulger bounced around Hollywood for the next&nbsp;
decade or so. Her most recognizable role is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.640
probably being Aunt Judy in the "Zenon"&nbsp;
trilogy—Disney’s three-part television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.640
film installment in the late 90s and&nbsp;
early 2000s. Fulger hasn't appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.280
in a mainstream production since a 2006&nbsp;
guest spot on the series "Without a Trace."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:12.160
This is because she's shifted her focus&nbsp;
from acting to tackling beauty standards.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.320
In 2012, Fulger started a web&nbsp;
series called "Speaking of Beauty."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.920
This spun off into a book titled "Speaking of&nbsp;
Beauty's Inner Beauty Journal." Fulger says,

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.000
“[It’s] a guide to help women recognize&nbsp;
their individuality defined their beauty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.320
not their hair, makeup, and body size."

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.040
This in turn led to the 2016 YouTube&nbsp;
series "The Hollywood Beauty Detective,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.920
and her current endeavor,&nbsp;
True Beauty Discovery. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.320
a nonprofit that aims to celebrate the&nbsp;
diverse beauty reflected in all of us.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.840
Fulger explained what spurred this&nbsp;
career development and passion for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.720
speaking on women’s beauty in an interview with&nbsp;
cleveland.com earlier this year. She shared,

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:52.960
"One of the things that I was always affected&nbsp;
by when I was a kid is I wanted to look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.600
beautiful women in magazines and on TV.&nbsp;
I didn't. I had buck teeth, braces and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.640
glasses that were so thick that my eyes looked&nbsp;
gigantic [...] It affected my career, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.680
as I became an actress and I started working, it&nbsp;
was still the question of what do you look like?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:12.240
I worked a lot and did wonderful projects, but&nbsp;
yet there's always that 'not enough' feeling."

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.480
This inspired her to dive into beauty when&nbsp;
she got older and had a kid of her own.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.520
Phil Buckman has had something of a&nbsp;
multi-hyphenate career. It's hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.480
identify him with any one project. He's been&nbsp;
around for a few decades, doing various jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:30.880
across diverse realms in the entertainment&nbsp;
industry. Some may remember him as Vic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.240
the zealously loyal intern from "Bob Patterson,&nbsp;
"Jason Alexander's short-lived first shot at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.040
a post-"Seinfeld" career. Others may just&nbsp;
recognize him from a handful of other titles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.520
including guest appearances on shows like&nbsp;
“Mom.” Buckham has been around the block.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.080
Even so, most have likely heard Buckman's voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.080
somewhere. He spent years doing voiceover&nbsp;
work, and he's done promos for Fox shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.760
like "The Orville." He also does&nbsp;
vocal work in multiple video games.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.640
Outside of voice acting and the occasional guest&nbsp;
spot, Buckman is an accomplished rock bassist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.400
He spent a spell playing bass for industrial rock&nbsp;
band Filter, and is currently the touring bassist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:12.640
for Fuel. He's also a member of Petty Cash, a hard&nbsp;
rocking cover band made up of touring musicians.

00:09:13.853 --> 00:09:20.320
Staci Keanan spent her youth on three sitcoms.&nbsp;
From 1987 to 1990, she starred on "My Two Dads"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:25.760
as Nicole, the child with the titular&nbsp;
double dad situation. From 1990 to 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:31.200
she starred on the long-forgotten TGIF sitcom&nbsp;
"Going Places." She then got her biggest role yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.720
as Dana Foster on "Step By Step." She played the&nbsp;
wisecracking young woman for all seven seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.520
from 1991 to 1998. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
she's acted sparsely since then.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.080
She’s now practicing law. She got her&nbsp;
J.D. at Southwestern Law School in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.080
spent the next three years as Deputy&nbsp;
District Attorney for the Riverside DA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:56.000
and joined the Los Angeles DA's Office&nbsp;
in 2016. In 2021, she joined the faculty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.640
of Southwestern Law School as an adjunct&nbsp;
professor. Her faculty bio lists her many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.720
legal accomplishments in detail, only&nbsp;
adding this brief mention at the end:

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.480
"Before beginning her law career,&nbsp;
Professor Sagorsky had a career in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.680
entertainment industry and worked as an actress&nbsp;
on several primetime network television shows."

00:10:14.722 --> 00:10:19.120
Aaron Michael Metchik played Steven Floyd&nbsp;
Torkelson, the sports-minded brother of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:24.480
titular family in "The Torkelsons." Fans of the&nbsp;
first season remember him clearly. Fans of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:29.440
second season never saw him after his character&nbsp;
was retconned out after the show was retooled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.360
He popped up in various other shows as&nbsp;
one of the seemingly endless child actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.920
the '90s threw at us before largely&nbsp;
vanishing from our screens after Y2K.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.080
He still makes his living in acting, though&nbsp;
— but now, primarily as an acting coach.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.960
Aaron's mother Robyn Metchik was an influential&nbsp;
drama teacher in the Arroyo Grande area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:51.280
so it's only natural her son went on to&nbsp;
become an actor. Aaron Metchik’s website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:56.480
boasts many success stories, but there's one&nbsp;
that looms above all else. In the mid 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:00.480
his mother had a talented student in&nbsp;
her drama class, one she realized had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:06.160
a ton of potential. This happened to be a&nbsp;
very, very familiar star named Zac Efron.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.960
Heard of him? Robyn Metchik set him up with an&nbsp;
agent, and Aaron became his private acting coach.

00:11:12.237 --> 00:11:16.480
Melinda Mullins played Hilary Booth on&nbsp;
"Remember WENN," the AMC show about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:22.400
goings-on of a radio station in the 1930s and&nbsp;
'40s. Hilary, an aloof former stage actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.960
played the female lead in many of the station's&nbsp;
shows. The show has largely left the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.000
imagination despite the involvement of Rupert&nbsp;
Holmes and being an early original work of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.960
now dominant TV channel. Mullins, also known&nbsp;
for playing Andrea in "Dennis the Menace,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.440
hasn't been seen on screen since&nbsp;
"Remember WENN" got cancelled in 1998.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:46.880
After the show's cancellation, Mullins was mulling&nbsp;
retirement when she met fellow actor Joshua Bryant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:51.840
at a film festival. The two fell in love, retired&nbsp;
together, and went to live in the New Mexico&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:57.360
desert. They largely keep to themselves and their&nbsp;
horses, but still take part in local theater.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:03.360
Grizz, played by and named after Grizz&nbsp;
Chapman, was the most physically imposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.480
of Tracy's entourage in "30 Rock" — and the&nbsp;
most sensitive. "30 Rock" was Chapman's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.120
credited acting role on screen, and remains his&nbsp;
most prominent. He's appeared in a couple dozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.760
shows and movies since, almost always in a&nbsp;
bouncer or bodyguard-type role. Besides the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.920
possibility of typecasting and limited&nbsp;
roles for anyone over seven feet tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.240
Chapman hasn't had too many roles lately&nbsp;
because he has other business endeavors.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:31.680
Chapman co-owns Bronx-based comic shop The&nbsp;
Lair, and frequents comic cons as both a vendor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:36.480
and a fan. He also hosts the UndaDog&nbsp;
podcast, where he interviews musicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.120
actors, and various other personalities.&nbsp;
Guests include Jon Hamm, Adam Richman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:46.880
and former "30 Rock" co-star Scott Adsit. Chapman&nbsp;
also has a Cameo account. So, if you miss seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:51.920
him on your screen, you can pay him $30 and&nbsp;
he'll address you personally to get your fix.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:52.480
“You’re the best.”

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:58.240
John Lutz played J.D. Lutz, the biggest&nbsp;
loser in the room of losers that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:04.080
TGS writing staff on "30 Rock." A huge&nbsp;
dork despised by everyone around him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.120
Lutz was often a victim of the Pranksmen and&nbsp;
the source of Liz and Jack's frequent ire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:14.720
The real Lutz is far more liked, but he does use&nbsp;
"unpopular dweeb" as part of his comic persona.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:20.000
Outside "Mapleworth Murders," a show he co-wrote&nbsp;
and starred in for Quibi, he hasn't been in much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.600
since. But fans of late night television have&nbsp;
still seen him fairly often post-"30 Rock."

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:30.560
Lutz was always more of a writer than an actor,&nbsp;
and as such returned to that world after "30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:35.280
Rock" wrapped. He almost immediately joined the&nbsp;
inaugural writing staff of "Late Night with Seth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:40.160
Meyers" and remains there to this day. He also&nbsp;
plays versions of himself, like in a "Late Night"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:44.960
bit where he is sent a green screen for Zoom but&nbsp;
instead uses a picture of his own living room. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:49.520
of his most common recurring bits is the "Leave&nbsp;
Him Alone Guy," an audience member who sticks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:54.720
up for the oft-despicable public figures Meyers&nbsp;
mocks and offers himself as an alternative target.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:56.560
“Leave him alone!”

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:02.000
Leila Kenzle played Fran Devanow ,&nbsp;
the '90s sitcom mandated Neurotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.880
Best Friend on "Mad About You." Proximity&nbsp;
to Helen Hunt and Paul Reiser on a popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:10.320
sitcom should have guaranteed something&nbsp;
of a career after the series wrapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.440
but show business in that era wasn't suited&nbsp;
for her. She told the New York Post in 2007,

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.840
"I was 37 and the idea of having to&nbsp;
parade myself in front of 27-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:23.920
executives began to feel a bit silly. I&nbsp;
wanted to be treated like a grown-up."

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.400
And so, she changed careers. Kenzle&nbsp;
did the odd guest spot here and there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:33.360
all while attending Antioch University. She&nbsp;
graduated with a Masters in Psychology in 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:36.080
and now works as a therapist in Los Angeles.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:40.720
Of course, her old show is still in&nbsp;
syndication, which can cause issues. She shared,

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:42.640
"If patients recognize me right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:46.560
it takes some time to figure out&nbsp;
who they're meeting — me or Fran."

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.880
She is so very involved in her practice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:52.880
Kenzle didn't even return for the&nbsp;
"Mad About You" revival in 2019.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:59.360
Johnny Kastl played Dr. Doug Murphy on&nbsp;
"Scrubs." Playing a fatally inept doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:03.120
turned successful pathologist proved&nbsp;
somewhat prescient about his personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:07.520
life — specifically the part about a career&nbsp;
change. After "Scrubs" wrapped, he appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:12.160
on a half dozen episodes of the Patrick Swayze&nbsp;
vehicle "The Beast" before retiring from acting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.240
He hasn't had another screen credit&nbsp;
since, because he's gone into law.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:21.360
Not long after leaving "The Beast," Kastl&nbsp;
enrolled at the University of Iowa College of Law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.440
Starting around 2010, when he was a&nbsp;
2L, he clerked at the U.S. Attorney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:29.040
Office for the Northern District of&nbsp;
Iowa. He later spent time practicing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:33.200
law at various firms and companies&nbsp;
before co-founding Acorn Finance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:38.800
where he serves as VP of Business Development.&nbsp;
In 2019, Kastl told the Chicago Tribune,

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.640
"Law was always the end goal. When 'Scrubs'&nbsp;
was winding down, we had reached a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.480
where we wanted to have kids and it seemed&nbsp;
like the right time for the exit strategy."

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:51.840
When asked if he ever saw himself acting again,&nbsp;
he said that, if so, it wouldn’t be immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.440
but eventually there’s a possibility. He&nbsp;
spent some time on the Victory Gardens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:59.360
Theater board of directors, never&nbsp;
fully leaving show business behind.

